  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM EDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis:\n\nRidging will continue to slide over the Great Lakes this evening and \ntonight, eventually departing east Tuesday as a seasonably strong \nshortwave trough punches overhead by Tuesday night. The warm front \ncurrently draped mainly west/east across the Straits will gradually \nlift north with time tonight. Meanwhile, the parent cyclone tied to \nthe aforementioned shortwave will trek across south-central Canada \nthrough the first half of the week, potentially swinging a diffuse \ncold front into the area in the Wednesday/Wednesday night timeframe. \nExpansive ridging is then expected to amplify across much of center \nof the continent late this week and through this weekend.\n\nForecast Details:\n\nWidespread rain from earlier this morning has moved on well to the \nsoutheast this afternoon with only a few additional showers/storms \npopping up in the wake near Saginaw Bay. Expectation is that the \nvast majority of northern Michigan will remain dry this afternoon \nand evening with partly to mostly clear skies at sunset. Another \nround of showers/storms is possible later tonight. Additional \nconvection is likely to initiate across north-central Wisconsin this \nevening along/ahead of the front, eventually working into northern \nMichigan very late this evening and tonight. Thunderstorm intensity \nupon arrival is in question, but elevated buoyancy in excess of \n1,000 J/kg and ~30 kts of deep-layer shear could support a strong \nstorm or two -- and this aligns with a marginal risk (1/5) for \nsevere storms in the latest convective outlook from SPC. A quick \n0.5\"-1\"+ of additional rainfall will still be possible should \norganized storms move across the area tonight given near-\nclimatological max PWATs in place to support efficient rainfall.\n\nWhile rain-free, warm weather is expected for most of northern \nMichigan on Tuesday, a few pop-up showers and storms cannot be \ncompletely ruled out across northeast lower. That said, mostly to \npartly sunny skies with highs climbing well into the 80s -- and even \nclose to 90 locally -- are in store for the afternoon. Widespread \nrain and embedded thunder chances return to the forecast Wednesday \nas aforementioned troughing slides over the Great Lakes. With \nridging expected to build behind this, rain chances appear low late \nthis week and through much of the upcoming holiday weekend.",
